WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.460
It's pretty clear what Cocaine Bear is&nbsp;
about. But who's in it? Who's behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:10.800
it? And why did this true story need to&nbsp;
be re-told? Beyond the bonkers premise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.220
here's everything you need&nbsp;
to know about Cocaine Bear.

00:00:14.933 --> 00:00:15.840
Cocaine Bear was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:21.240
first announced in 2019, with the trade&nbsp;
describing it as "a unique bear-driven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:25.380
horror comedy — with heart." That's&nbsp;
quite the summary, and since then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:31.380
we've been expectantly waiting to see the story of&nbsp;
"Pablo Escobear" play out on the big screen. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:38.160
we know exactly when that big furry predator will&nbsp;
start snorting and snarling and scaring its poor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:44.100
victims. Universal released the first poster for&nbsp;
the project in November 2022, one that pictured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:50.520
a black-and-white image of a very coked-up bear&nbsp;
looking ready to seriously mess some people up.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:54.900
The poster's tagline reads "get in&nbsp;
line," and it was accompanied by a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.900 --> 00:01:00.480
release date. Prepare to see the horrors&nbsp;
and hilarity of a drug-fueled man-eater&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:05.940
when Cocaine Bear crashes exclusively&nbsp;
into theaters on February 24, 2023.

00:01:06.772 --> 00:01:08.100
The plot of Cocaine Bear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:12.000
promises to be absolutely wild.&nbsp;
According to The Hollywood Reporter,

00:01:30.282 --> 00:01:34.500
Cocaine Bear will mix horror and comedy&nbsp;
together, creating a movie that'll deliver&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:39.540
both cackles and carnage. Of course, one of&nbsp;
the biggest selling points for the film is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:44.820
that it's based on a true story. According to&nbsp;
Rolling Stone, in real life, a man named Andrew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:49.200
C. Thornton decided to use his background&nbsp;
as a paratrooper, cop, and lawyer — along&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:54.240
with his own plane — to smuggle cocaine from&nbsp;
Colombia into the U.S. During one fateful run,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:59.580
he overloaded the plane and was forced to toss&nbsp;
packages of coke down into the forest below.

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:06.360
That's when some poor bear stumbled upon the nose&nbsp;
candy and decided to gobble down about $15 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:11.580
of the stuff. Needless to say, the bear&nbsp;
overdosed. As for Thornton, his fate was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:16.500
also pretty grisly. Realizing he would need to&nbsp;
bail, Thornton jumped from his plane ... only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:21.300
his parachute didn't open, and some unlucky&nbsp;
fellow found Thornton dead on his driveway.

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:25.380
"An elderly gentleman had called&nbsp;
the dispatch and said that there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:29.160
was somebody in his yard and he&nbsp;
didn't look like he was moving."

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:33.900
The story was reported on by The New York&nbsp;
Times, and now, this larger-than-life tale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:37.800
is getting what promises to be an&nbsp;
even crazier big-screen adaptation.

00:02:38.483 --> 00:02:40.260
Just like the titular bear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:46.860
is absolutely loaded with coke, Cocaine Bear&nbsp;
is absolutely loaded with talent. First up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:50.340
there's Keri Russell, best known for&nbsp;
playing Russian spy Elizabeth Jennings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:55.680
in The Americans and the titular character in&nbsp;
J.J. Abrams' WB series Felicity. Interestingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.680 --> 00:03:00.780
Russell won't be the only Americans alum in the&nbsp;
film, as she'll be joined by Margo Martindale, who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:06.180
won two Emmys for playing Russell's KGB handler in&nbsp;
the aforementioned FX series, as well as Matthew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:11.160
Rhys, who starred in the series as Russell's&nbsp;
husband/fellow undercover spy, Philip Jennings.

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:16.200
They'll be accompanied by the late, great Ray&nbsp;
Liotta, who starred in films like Goodfellas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:21.540
and Field of Dreams. As the actor passed away&nbsp;
in May 2022, Cocaine Bear will be the legendary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:27.120
actor's final film. The movie also stars Alden&nbsp;
Ehrenreich of Solo: A Star Wars Story fame,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:32.160
O'Shea Jackson Jr. from Straight Outta Compton,&nbsp;
and Jesse Tyler Ferguson of Modern Family.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:34.560
"Bears can't climb trees."

00:03:34.753 --> 00:03:36.013
"Of course they can."

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:41.580
In keeping with its reported horror-comedy vibe,&nbsp;
Cocaine Bear was originally going to be directed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:45.660
by Radio Silence — the group comprised&nbsp;
of Matt Bettinelli-Olpin, Tyler Gillett,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:50.460
and Chad Villella, three guys who've helmed&nbsp;
similarly hilarious yet scary films like Ready&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:57.000
or Not and Scream 6. However, now Radio Silence is&nbsp;
out, and another director skilled at comedy is in.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:01.320
Cocaine Bear will now be helmed by the&nbsp;
one and only Elizabeth Banks. On the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:05.520
acting side of things, you know Banks from&nbsp;
The Hunger Games franchise and the Pitch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:09.180
Perfect series. On the directing side&nbsp;
of things, Banks has two movies under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:14.100
her filmmaking belt — Pitch Perfect 2 and&nbsp;
2019's Charlie's Angels. In other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:21.960
we know for a 100% fact that Banks can bring the&nbsp;
laughs. As for the thrills, we're very interested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:26.760
to see what Banks can bring to the horror&nbsp;
table as she branches out from her usual genre.

00:04:27.473 --> 00:04:29.160
In addition to Elizabeth Banks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:33.720
Cocaine Bear has quite a bit of talent&nbsp;
sitting behind the camera. The film's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:39.420
producers are absolute powerhouses when it comes&nbsp;
to creating zany, mind-bending movies. Phil Lord&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:43.800
and Chris Miller have been involved with the&nbsp;
project since 2019. The two are perhaps best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:47.760
known for their directing work, having helmed&nbsp;
films like Cloudy With a Chance of Meatballs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:52.020
21 Jump Street, and The LEGO Movie.&nbsp;
They've also produced quite a few winners,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:56.580
including The Mitchells vs. the Machines&nbsp;
and Spider-Man: Into the Spider-Verse.

00:04:56.580 --> 00:05:01.860
Of course, they aren't the only ones producing&nbsp;
this cocaine-fueled pic. In addition to Aditya&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:06.000
Sood and Max Handelman, Brian Duffield will help&nbsp;
bring this movie to the big screen. Duffield&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:10.920
is one of the most original filmmakers working&nbsp;
today. Having penned scripts for titles like The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:16.260
Babysitter, Underwater, and Spontaneous, Duffield&nbsp;
is definitely skilled at combining comedy with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:22.348
jaw-dropping horror. In other words, he seems&nbsp;
like the perfect fit for this insane project.

00:05:23.180 --> 00:05:24.840
This probably won't come as much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:27.360
of a shock, but Cocaine Bear is going to feature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:31.560
some drug use. It's right there in&nbsp;
the title. And once that titular apex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:37.500
predator starts snorting that coke, things&nbsp;
are going to get bloody — very, very bloody.

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:42.420
After all, movies that feature very hungry bears,&nbsp;
a la titles like Backcountry and The Revenant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:46.500
tend to get pretty intense – and those guys&nbsp;
weren't even using when they decided to hunt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:51.300
down some humans. Throw some nose candy into&nbsp;
the mix, and it should come as no surprise that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:58.200
Cocaine Bear has been rated R for "bloody violence&nbsp;
and gore, drug content, and language throughout."

00:05:58.482 --> 00:05:59.900
"Oh man, you f—ed."

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:02.756
"What the f–k is wrong with that bear?"

